// Expected: u0 at one session and at two.
//
// Two pseudonyms derived from one long-term seed and two per-session
// randomizers. They do share a secret origin, but the attacker never holds
// that seed, and `origin_leaves` only reports a link when the common ancestor
// is a secret the attacker can actually reach. So the query holds.
//
// What this pins is that replication does not manufacture a link witness.
// Doubling the principals doubles the observable pseudonyms and hands the
// witness search a much larger field to find a coincidence in, and the
// unlinkability decision procedure is the one most able to mistake structural
// similarity for a link. Two sessions of a role are two *different* people as
// far as this query is concerned; nothing about seeing more of them should
// make the first session's two pseudonyms linkable to each other.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private seed_k
	generates rand_a
	generates rand_b
	pseudo_a = HASH(seed_k, rand_a)
	pseudo_b = HASH(seed_k, rand_b)
]

Alice -> Bob: pseudo_a, pseudo_b, rand_a, rand_b

principal Bob[
	digest_v = HASH(pseudo_a, pseudo_b)
]

queries[
	unlinkability? pseudo_a, pseudo_b
]
